-- title:
--   Theorem 1.1 + 1.2 — a 3D-local CSS stabilizer Hamiltonian family is a self-correcting quantum memory (corrected: t_mem uses "> 1/10")
-- statement:
--   There exist a family of CSS codes $C_k$ ($k\in\mathbb N$) on $n_k$ qubits, positions of their qubits in $\mathbb R^3$, and constants $r$, $D$, $w$, $c_1,c_2>0$, $k_0$, $\beta_c>0$, $c>0$, $\eta>0$, all independent of $k$, such that:
--
--   1. $e^{c_1k}\le n_k\le e^{c_2k}$ for all $k\ge k_0$, i.e. $n_k=\exp(\Theta(k))$;
--   2. every $C_k$ encodes exactly one logical qubit;
--   3. every $C_k$ is local in $\mathbb R^3$: each Hamiltonian term is supported in a ball of radius $r$, every unit ball contains at most $D$ qubits, and every qubit is in at most $w$ checks;
--   4. for every $k$ there are logical operators, a decoder and a code isometry, defining the thermal encoding $\mathrm{Enc}_\beta$ and the decoding $\mathrm{Dec}$, such that for every inverse temperature $\beta>\beta_c$ and every Lindbladian $\mathcal L$ with admissible jump operators (single-qubit Pauli times a syndrome function) and $\mathcal L(\rho_\beta)=0$: $\mathrm{Enc}_\beta$ is CPTP, and for every single-qubit density matrix $\tau$,
--      - (a) $\mathrm{Dec}\circ\mathrm{Enc}_\beta(\tau)=\tau$, and for every $t\ge0$
--      - (b) $\displaystyle\bigl\|\Phi_t\circ\mathrm{Enc}_\beta(\tau)-\mathrm{Enc}_\beta(\tau)\bigr\|_1\le\|\mathcal L\|_1\,t\,e^{-c\,n_k^\eta}$,
--      - (c) if $\|\mathcal L\|_1t\le e^{c\,n_k^\eta}/10$, then $\bigl\|\Phi_t\circ\mathrm{Enc}_\beta(\tau)-\mathrm{Enc}_\beta(\tau)\bigr\|_1\le 1/10$,
--
--      where $\Phi_t=e^{-it[H_k,\cdot]+t\mathcal L}$ and $H_k$ is the stabilizer Hamiltonian of $C_k$.
--
--   Clause (c) says that the memory lifetime, the first time at which the encoded state has moved by more than $1/10$ in trace distance, is at least $e^{c\,n_k^\eta}/(10\|\mathcal L\|_1)$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: 𝓛 generated by local jump operators of O(1) support (p. 4); jump operators in the set 𝓔 of single-qubit Paulis (p. 21, footnote 7) → finitely many jumps S_j = P_j F_j with P_j a single-qubit Pauli and F_j a syndrome function, and 𝓛(ρ_β) = 0; reason: jumps literally in 𝓔 give a unital 𝓛, and for β ≠ 0 𝓛(ρ_β) = 0 then forces every jump to commute with H (Tr ρ_β Pρ_β P ≤ Tr ρ_β² with equality iff [P, ρ_β] = 0), so only Paulis that move no syndrome are allowed (nearly vacuous); Davies generators have the form P·F; multi-qubit jumps are not covered by the paper's decoder analysis (footnote 7, Definition 5.1)
--   - generalization: ‖Φ_{1/T,t}∘Enc_k(ρ_L) − Enc_k(ρ_L)‖₁ ≤ t exp(−Θ(n_k^η)), jump rates implicit → ≤ ‖𝓛‖₁ t e^{−c n_k^η} with β_c, c, η independent of 𝓛; reason: the form of eq. (48); with O(1) rates on O(n_k) jumps ‖𝓛‖₁ ≤ C n_k, which gives (3) with a smaller c
--   - reading: n_k = exp(Θ(k)); exp(−Θ(n_k^η)) in an upper bound; O(1) radius and density; "independent of k" → ∃ c₁ c₂ > 0, k₀ with e^{c₁k} ≤ n_k ≤ e^{c₂k} for k ≥ k₀; e^{−c n_k^η} with c > 0; constants r, D quantified before k; reason: standard meaning of Θ and O(1) for a family indexed by k
--   - generalization: there exist encoding and decoding maps Enc_k, Dec_k ("The encoding that we choose is a thermal encoding") → Enc_β is the thermal encoding (49), asserted CPTP as in Theorem 2.6, and Dec the partial trace in a decoder frame chosen per k before β and 𝓛; reason: a fixed explicit pair witnesses the existential and cannot depend on β, 𝓛 or τ
--   - generalization: LDPC (p. 10) and z_v = 7 (p. 22), not part of Theorems 1.1–1.2 → extra conjunct: every qubit is in at most w checks; reason: satisfied by the paper's codes; an extra conjunct in an existential is a stronger statement
--   - encoding: T < T_c with T_c > 0 → β > β_c with 0 < β_c; reason: β = 1/T
--   - encoding: an embedding of the qubits into ℝ³; a Hamiltonian term → a position map Fin n_k → ℝ³ (not required injective); a term is one row of H_x or H_z; reason: the paper does not claim injectivity; H has one term per check
--   - correction: t_mem = min{t : ‖Φ_{1/T,t}∘Enc_k(ρ_L) − Enc_k(ρ_L)‖₁ ≤ 1/10} ≥ exp(Θ(n_k^η)) (eqs. (4)–(5)) → for all t ≥ 0, ‖𝓛‖₁ t ≤ e^{c n_k^η}/10 implies ‖Φ_t∘Enc_β(τ) − Enc_β(τ)‖₁ ≤ 1/10; reason: counterexample: at t = 0 the distance is 0 ≤ 1/10 for every code and state, so the printed t_mem is 0 and (4) is false for every family; the text ("the timescale when the encoded state is forgotten", "(2) follows trivially from (1)") fixes the intended "> 1/10"
-- source:
--   Balasubramanian, Davydova, Lin, A passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions, arXiv:2605.10943, Theorem 1.1 and Theorem 1.2, p. 4; formal version Theorem 3.16, p. 40; setting of §2.5, pp. 19–21

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_CSSCode_gibbsState
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_evolution
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_superNorm
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_IsAdmissibleJump
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_encoding
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_decoding
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_IsCPTP
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_IsLocal
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_HasDensity
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_QubitDegreeLE
open scoped ComplexOrder

namespace SelfCorrect3D.Memory

/-- **Theorems 1.1 and 1.2: a passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions**
(corrected: `t_mem` uses "> 1/10"). Balasubramanian, Davydova, Lin, *A passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions*, arXiv:2605.10943, Theorem 1.1 and Theorem 1.2,
p. 4; formal version Theorem 3.16, p. 40; setting of §2.5, pp. 19–21.

There are a family of CSS codes `C_k` on `n_k` qubits, qubit positions in ℝ³ and constants
`r, D, w, c₁, c₂ > 0, k₀, β_c > 0, c > 0, η > 0`, independent of `k`, such that
1. `e^{c₁k} ≤ n_k ≤ e^{c₂k}` for `k ≥ k₀` (`n_k = exp(Θ(k))`);
2. every `C_k` encodes exactly one logical qubit;
3. every `C_k` is local in ℝ³ with radius `r`, has at most `D` qubits per unit ball and at most `w`
   checks per qubit;
4. for every `k` there are logicals, a decoder and a code isometry `F` such that for every
   `β > β_c` and every Lindbladian `𝓛` of admissible jump operators with `𝓛(ρ_β) = 0`: the thermal
   encoding `Enc_β` is CPTP, and for every single-qubit density matrix `τ`,
   (a) `Dec(Enc_β(τ)) = τ`, and for every `t ≥ 0`
   (b) `‖Φ_t(Enc_β τ) − Enc_β τ‖₁ ≤ ‖𝓛‖₁ t e^{−c n_k^η}`,
   (c) if `‖𝓛‖₁ t ≤ e^{c n_k^η}/10` then `‖Φ_t(Enc_β τ) − Enc_β τ‖₁ ≤ 1/10`,
   where `Φ_t = e^{−it[H_k,·] + t𝓛}`.

**Formalization Note.** Readings, all recorded in the item's differences list: (1) admissible
jump operators are `P · F`, `P` a single-qubit Pauli, `F` a syndrome function
(`IsAdmissibleJump`), with stationary `ρ_β`; (2) the rate dependence is explicit through
`‖𝓛‖₁` (`superNorm` of the dissipator), with `β_c, c, η` independent of `𝓛`; (3) `Θ(·)` and
`O(1)` become explicit constants independent of `k`; (4) `Enc_k`, `Dec_k` are the thermal
encoding (49) and partial trace in the frame `F`, fixed per `k` before `β` and `𝓛`; (5) the
qubit-degree bound is an extra conjunct; (6) `T < T_c` is `β > β_c = 1/T_c`; positions are a map
`Fin n_k → ℝ³` and a Hamiltonian term is a row of `H_x` or `H_z`. (Correction) Eq. (5) defines
`t_mem = min{t : ‖…‖₁ ≤ 1/10}`, which is `0` for every code (the distance is `0` at `t = 0`);
clause (c) states the intended lifetime bound ("the timescale when the encoded state is
forgotten", i.e. the first time the distance exceeds `1/10`). -/
theorem exists_3d_self_correcting_memory :
    ∃ (n mX mZ : ℕ → ℕ) (C : ∀ k, CSSCode (n k) (mX k) (mZ k))
      (pos : ∀ k, Fin (n k) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 3))
      (r : ℝ) (D w : ℕ) (c₁ c₂ : ℝ) (k₀ : ℕ) (βc c η : ℝ),
      0 < c₁ ∧ 0 < c₂ ∧ 0 < βc ∧ 0 < c ∧ 0 < η ∧
      (∀ k ≥ k₀, Real.exp (c₁ * k) ≤ n k ∧ (n k : ℝ) ≤ Real.exp (c₂ * k)) ∧
      (∀ k, (C k).OneLogicalQubit) ∧
      (∀ k, IsLocal (C k) (pos k) r ∧ HasDensity (pos k) D ∧ QubitDegreeLE (C k) w) ∧
      ∀ k, ∃ F : MemoryFrame (C k), ∀ β : ℝ, βc < β →
        ∀ (m : ℕ) (J : Fin m → Op (n k)), (∀ j, IsAdmissibleJump (C k) (J j)) →
          dissipator J ((C k).gibbsState β) = 0 →
          IsCPTP (encoding (C k) F β) ∧
          ∀ τ : Matrix (ZMod 2) (ZMod 2) ℂ, τ.PosSemidef → τ.trace = 1 →
            decoding (C k) F (encoding (C k) F β τ) = τ ∧
            ∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t →
              WildeQIT.traceNorm (evolution (C k).hamiltonian J t (encoding (C k) F β τ) -
                  encoding (C k) F β τ) ≤
                superNorm (dissipator J) * t * Real.exp (-(c * (n k : ℝ) ^ η)) ∧
              (superNorm (dissipator J) * t ≤ Real.exp (c * (n k : ℝ) ^ η) / 10 →
                WildeQIT.traceNorm (evolution (C k).hamiltonian J t (encoding (C k) F β τ) -
                  encoding (C k) F β τ) ≤ 1 / 10) := by sorry

end SelfCorrect3D.Memory
